              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0800:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Raymond Miller, a 59-year-old male, admitted from the oncology clinic. Diagnosed with extensive-stage small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) 3 weeks ago with bulky mediastinal and bilateral hilar masses. Over the past 48 hours, his spouse noted progressive lethargy, confusion, slurred speech, generalized weakness, and nausea with three episodes of vomiting. On assessment: client is somnolent, responds slowly to verbal stimuli, oriented only to self. Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score: 12 (E3 V4 M5). Neuromuscular exam reveals coarse fasciculations in quadriceps and bilateral depressed patellar reflexes (+1). Cardiovascular: heart sounds regular, S1/S2, no murmurs. Lungs: scattered rhonchi in right upper zone. Abdomen soft, non-tender. Skin warm, turgid; no peripheral edema in extremities. Stat lab work reveals critical serum sodium of 116 mmol/L. Interprofessional care team notified.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0900:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Critical lab results verified with hospital laboratory. Serum sodium is 116 mmol/L (critical low). Serum osmolality is 242 mOsm/kg (hypoosmolar). Concurrently, urine osmolality is 580 mOsm/kg (inappropriately high) and urine sodium is 56 mmol/L. Serum calcium is normal (2.32 mmol/L). Serum potassium is 4.1 mmol/L. Cortisol level normal. Findings confirm paraneoplastic Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH) secondary to small cell lung cancer. Seizure precautions instituted.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1000:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Medical orders initiated. Raymond placed on strict fluid restriction of 800 mL/24 hours (all oral fluids, IV flushes, and medication diluents counted). Hypertonic 3% NaCl ordered at 25 mL/hr via central line on smart infusion pump, with goal of raising serum sodium by 4 to 6 mmol/L over the first 6 hours, not to exceed 8 mmol/L total rise in 24 hours. Stat point-of-care serum sodium checks scheduled every 2 hours. Padded side rails, suction canister, and emergency bag-valve-mask checked at bedside for seizure precautions.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1200:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Hypertonic 3% NaCl infusing at 25 mL/hr via dedicated central line lumen. Raymond's fluid restriction sign posted. Thirst managed with small ice chips sparingly measured into the 800 mL allowance and oral moisturizing swabs. Raymond remains somnolent but arouses more easily; able to state his name and birth month. GCS: 13 (E4 V4 M5). Neurological checks stable; no tremors or seizure activity observed. Point-of-care serum sodium at 1200: 119 mmol/L (rise of 3 mmol/L from baseline 116 mmol/L). Rate of infusion maintained.</span></p>"
