              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0845:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Tyler Brooks, 34, admitted via trauma stretcher following an enclosed-space gasoline vapor explosion. Weight: 80 kg. Primary survey (CAB/ABC): Airway: client is awake, speaking in a raspy, strained whisper. Facial burns present; eyebrows and nasal hairs completely singed. Oral cavity inspection reveals carbonaceous soot on hard palate, posterior pharynx, and uvula, with mild edematous swelling of the buccal mucosa. Auscultation of the trachea reveals audible high-pitched inspiratory stridor; bilateral lung fields exhibit scattered expiratory wheezes. Breathing: tachypneic at 28 breaths/min; 100% non-rebreather mask applied. Circulation: peripheral pulses thready at 124 beats/min; blood pressure 98/62 mm Hg. Severe burns covering the anterior torso and bilateral entire upper extremities with mixed areas of blistered red dermis and dry, waxy, leathery eschar. Neurological: GCS 14 (confused, anxious from hypoxia and pain). Trauma team alerted for immediate rapid sequence intubation.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0915:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Endotracheal intubation performed smoothly by trauma team prior to worsening laryngeal edema; tube secured at 23 cm at teeth. Mechanical ventilation initiated on 100% FiO<sub>2</sub> with lung-protective settings. Burn mapping verified: face (4.5%), anterior chest/abdomen (18%), right upper extremity circumference (9%), left upper extremity circumference (9%), totaling 40.5% TBSA (partial- and full-thickness). Total weight: 80 kg. Foley catheter placed, initial output 15 mL of dark, concentrated amber urine. Stat laboratory values received. Parkland resuscitation calculations initiated.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">0930:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Parkland fluid resuscitation active: 12,960 mL total LR planned for first 24 hours. Prehospital EMS infused 500 mL LR over 45 minutes; remaining first 8-hour requirement is 5,980 mL over the next 7 hours and 15 minutes (~825 mL/hr across two large-bore peripheral IV lines). Hourly Foley urometer measurements initiated. Core temperature maintained at 37.0° C with overhead radiant warmers and warm blankets. Burn wounds covered with clean, dry, sterile sheets; topical antimicrobials deferred until primary trauma survey and fluid resuscitation are stabilized.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1130 (Hour 3 Post-Injury):</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Tyler remains sedated and intubated on mechanical ventilation. Endotracheal suctioning yields moderate amount of soot-tinged mucus; bilateral breath sounds clear with scattered rhonchi. IV LR infusing at 825 mL/hr. Foley output over the past hour was 42 mL (0.52 mL/kg/hr, on target). Blood pressure: 104/66 mm Hg, HR 110 beats/min. Telemetry shows sinus tachycardia with narrow QRS and normal T waves. Ambient room temperature maintained at 31° C; patient's core temperature is 36.8° C. Clean dry dressings intact.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1445 (Hour 6 Post-Injury):</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Resuscitation parameters: Urine output for the past hour was 48 mL (cumulative 6-hour output: 245 mL, avg 41 mL/hr). Blood pressure 108/68 mm Hg, HR 102 beats/min. Bilateral radial pulses assessed via Doppler ultrasound: strong biphasic acoustic signals present; brisk capillary refill in nailbeds. Upper extremities elevated on pillows to promote venous return. Serum potassium trending down from 5.8 to 5.1 mmol/L; hematocrit stabilized at 48%. Lactate decreased to 2.4 mmol/L. Total LR infused in first 6 hours: 4,800 mL. On schedule to complete first half by Hour 8.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1500 (Hour 30 Post-Burn):</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Tyler has entered the diuretic phase. Spontaneous urine output has increased to 75–90 mL/hr. IV Lactated Ringer's infusion successfully stepped down to maintenance D5 0.45% NS with 20 mEq KCl/L. Serum sodium 138 mmol/L, potassium 3.8 mmol/L (hypokalemia monitored and replaced), hematocrit 36% (hemodilution). Arterial blood gas shows PaO<sub>2</sub> 98 mm Hg on FiO<sub>2</sub> 40% with PEEP 5 cm H<sub>2</sub>O. Weaning protocol initiated. Burn surgery team scheduled Tyler for tangential excision and autografting of deep partial-thickness arm burns tomorrow.</span></p>"
