        "leftContent": {
          "intro": "Karen is now on Post-Burn Day 3. The nurse collaborates with the burn team dietitian and surgeon to optimize nutritional support and track wound healing phases.",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "id": "nn_3",
              "title": "Nurses' Notes",
              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1600 (Hour 8 Post-Injury):</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Karen Foster, 42, transferred from the emergency department. Weight: 68 kg. Total burn size: 24% TBSA. Wounds: right thigh exhibits moist, hyperemic, blistering superficial partial-thickness burns (painful); right lower leg from knee to ankle has full-thickness circumferential third-degree burns with dry, white-tan, leathery, contracted eschar; right forearm exhibits circumferential deep partial-thickness burns. Fluid resuscitation per Parkland is infusing on target with urine output 35 mL/hr. During current neurovascular check of the right lower leg, client cries out in severe agony when toes are passively moved upward (pain with passive stretch). Right foot is pale and noticeably cooler than left foot. Capillary refill in right great toe is sluggish at 4–5 seconds. Palpation of the dorsalis pedis pulse is absent; Doppler probe reveals faint, monophasic signal. Calf is hard and unyielding. Burn surgeon immediately alerted for emergency bedside escharotomy.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1620:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Burn surgeon arrives at bedside. Right calf compartment pressure measured with Stryker needle: 38 mm Hg (abnormally elevated; normal &lt; 10–15 mm Hg; &gt; 30 mm Hg diagnostic of compartment syndrome). Right dorsalis pedis Doppler signal has completely ceased. Immediate bedside escharotomy performed under sterile conditions: longitudinal medial and lateral incisions made through the rigid, insensate eschar of the right lower leg down to subcutaneous fat. Upon incision, the eschar edges immediately gape open by 1.5 cm with visible decompression of underlying tissues. Bleeding controlled with electrocautery. Within 5 minutes, Doppler acoustic signal returns strongly in dorsalis pedis artery; toes become warm with capillary refill &lt; 2 seconds. Pain on passive stretch diminishes. Sterile non-adherent dressings applied.</span></p><p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1000 (Post-Burn Day 3):</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">Karen is resting in the burn step-down unit. Right leg escharotomy wounds show clean subcutaneous margins with healthy granulation tissue and strong palpable pedal pulses (+2). Right forearm deep partial-thickness burns being cleansed with chlorhexidine and dressed with silver sulfadiazine cream. Enteral nutrition infusing via small-bore nasojejunal tube at 75 mL/hr (target 2,400 kcal/day, 130 g protein/day) with good tolerance and no abdominal distension. Resting energy expenditure is elevated by 60% above predicted basal rate. Serum prealbumin is 110 mg/L (target &gt; 180 mg/L, reflecting ongoing catabolism). Blood glucose maintained between 7.0–10.0 mmol/L on sliding-scale insulin.</span></p>"
            },
            {
              "id": "vs_1",
              "title": "Vital Signs",
              "content": "<table class=\"nclex-editor-table\" style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:#025287; color:white; font-weight:600; text-align:left;\"></th><th style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:#025287; color:white; font-weight:600; text-align:left;\">Value (1600)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:#025287; color:white; font-weight:600; text-align:left;\">Target Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\"><b>Heart Rate</b></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">118 beats/min, sinus tachycardia</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">60 – 100 beats/min</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\"><b>Blood Pressure</b></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">132/86 mm Hg</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">&lt; 120/80 mm Hg</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\"><b>Respiratory Rate</b></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">22 breaths/min</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">12 – 20 breaths/min</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\"><b>Right Dorsalis Pedis Pulse</b></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">Faint Doppler only (Absent palpation)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">+2 Palpable</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\"><b>Right Foot Temperature</b></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">Cool, mottled pale</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccd8e0; padding:8px; background:white; color:#1e293b;\">Warm, pink</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            },
            {
              "id": "bc_2",
              "title": "Burn Classification Reference",
              "content": "<div style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\"><b>American Burn Association Depth Classification:</b></div><ul style=\"line-height:1.6;\"><li><b>1st Degree:</b> Epidermis only; red, dry, painful, no blisters.</li><li><b>2nd Degree (Superficial Partial):</b> Papillary dermis; blisters, moist, weeping, very painful.</li><li><b>2nd Degree (Deep Partial):</b> Reticular dermis; waxy-white mottled, less painful, sluggish blanch.</li><li><b>3rd Degree (Full Thickness):</b> Subcutaneous involvement; dry, leathery, charred, insensate, no blanching.</li><li><b>4th Degree:</b> Muscle, tendon, bone involvement; requires amputation or extensive flap coverage.</li></ul>"
            },
            {
              "id": "wh_3",
              "title": "Wound Healing Stages Reference",
              "content": "<div style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\"><b>Four Phases of Natural Wound Healing in Burns:</b></div><ul style=\"line-height:1.6;\"><li><b>Phase 1: Hemostasis:</b> Platelet aggregation, fibrin clot formation, provisional matrix.</li><li><b>Phase 2: Inflammation:</b> Neutrophil and macrophage infiltration, cytokine release (VEGF, TGF-&beta;, PDGF), clearing debris/bacteria.</li><li><b>Phase 3: Proliferation:</b> Fibroblast migration, collagen deposition, angiogenesis, epithelialization.</li><li><b>Phase 4: Remodeling:</b> Collagen cross-linking, matrix remodeling by MMPs, scar contracture maturation.</li></ul>"
            }
          ]
        }
