          "footnote": "Taken from the Maryland Next Gen NCLEX Test Bank Project, University of Maryland School of Nursing: &ldquo;Heart Failure&rdquo; medical-surgical faculty case study (Lisa Seldomridge, PhD, RN, CNE; Jennifer Hart, DNP, FNP-BC; Molly Dale, DNP, FNP-BC; Salisbury University, January 25, 2023). Available at https://www.nursing.umaryland.edu/mnwc/initiatives/nextgen-nclex/nextgen-nclex-library/ (Faculty Case Studies). Laboratory values shown in SI units.",
          "preamble": "The nurse has reviewed the Clinic Notes from Day 1 1030.",
          "explanation": "The new order for sacubitril/valsartan should be questioned because the client is already taking enalapril: an ACE inhibitor must be stopped at least 36 hours before sacubitril/valsartan is started, because the combination can cause angioedema. The client needs education about stopping digoxin, a potassium supplement for the low potassium, a dietitian consultation about the fluid restriction and potassium-rich foods, and a scale for daily weights.<br>The client should not start the new medication until the order is clarified, and an extra diuretic dose is not ordered and would worsen the hypokalemia."
