              "id": "nn_umd14",
              "title": "Nurses' Notes",
              "content": "<p class=\"nurse-note-row\"><span class=\"nurse-note-time\">1100:</span><span class=\"nurse-note-text\">A 25-year-old male client who has been HIV positive for 3 years is admitted with a dry cough and a fever of 101.0° F (38.3° C). Client has fatigue, shortness of breath, a respiratory rate of 28 breaths/min, and a pulse oximetry reading of 90% on room air. Breathing is slightly labored, with a tight feeling in the chest on deep inspiration. Has been on antiretroviral medications since the diagnosis of HIV but recently stopped taking them after being laid off from his job. Symptoms did not improve with over-the-counter medications after 3 days. IV of 0.9% sodium chloride started in the right arm at 75 mL/hr. Labs drawn. Bronchoalveolar lavage completed; cytology report pending.</span></p>"
